<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Multilingual Instruction Fine-Tuning (IFT) is essential for enabling large language models (LLMs) to generalize effectively across diverse linguistic and cultural contexts. However, the scarcity of high-quality multilingual training data and corresponding building method remains a critical bottleneck. While data selection has shown promise in English settings, existing methods often fail to generalize across languages due to reliance on simplistic heuristics or language-specific assumptions. In this work, we introduce Multilingual Data Quality and Diversity (M-DaQ), a novel method for improving LLMs multilinguality, by selecting high-quality and semantically diverse multilingual IFT samples. We further conduct the first systematic investigation of the Superficial Alignment Hypothesis (SAH) in multilingual setting. Empirical results across 18 languages demonstrate that models fine-tuned with M-DaQ method achieve significant performance gains over vanilla baselines over 60% win rate. Human evaluations further validate these gains, highlighting the increment of cultural points in the response. We release the M-DaQ code<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        <a href="https://github.com/zhaocorey/M-DaQ.git" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/zhaocorey/M-DaQ.git</a></span></span></span> to support future research.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>
Machine learning, data selection, multilingual dataset, Instruction Fine-Tuning, LLM alignment.

</div><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span><sup id="id3.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup> Huawei, Beijing, China   <sup id="id3.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> Huawei Canada, Montreal, Canada 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id3.3" class="ltx_sup">3</sup> University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, China
</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some machine learning methods<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> have been proposed for improving Multilingual Instruction Fine-Tuning (IFT) dataset, which plays a pivotal role in enabling large language models (LLMs) to perform effectively general-purpose tasks. However, constructing high-quality multilingual IFT datasets remains a significant challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and the associated data collection and curation processes are often prohibitively expensive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. For instance, in the Llama 3 IFT dataset, only 3.01% of samples are multilingual. Consequently, the performance of multilingual LLMs continues to be constrained by the scarcity of high-quality, linguistically diverse training data.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:310.5pt;">
<p id="S1.F1.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.F1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2509.15549v1/images/M-DAQ-main-graph-v6.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:334/170;" width="334" height="170" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<p id="S1.F1.3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.F1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text">(a) M-DaQ data improvement overview</span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:310.5pt;">
<p id="S1.F1.4.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.F1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2509.15549v1/images/radar_v14.png" id="S1.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:334/259;" width="334" height="259" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<p id="S1.F1.4.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.F1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text">(b) M-DaQ Model versus Vanilla Model performance</span></p>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a) Evolution of the M-DaQ algorithm and its advantages in cost efficiency, data quality, and diversity. Languages: En (English), Th (Thai), Ar (Arabic), Fr (French). (b) Impact of M-DaQ across 18 languages, measured by win rate improvement.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, data selection has emerged as a promising strategy to enhance IFT efficiency and performance—particularly in English settings. Empirical studies demonstrate that carefully selected small-scale datasets (e.g. 1K samples<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>) can achieve competitive performance compared to models trained on significantly larger corpora (e.g. 52K samples<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>). Two key factors underpin effective data selection: (1) preserving linguistic and task diversity<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and (2) prioritizing samples of higher intrinsic quality based on well-defined criteria<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Existing works in English IFT have explored various heuristics for <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">data quality</span> estimation. For example, Zhao et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> use IFT data complexity as a proxy for data quality. However, such simplistic heuristics may lack generalizability, especially in multilingual contexts where linguistic structures and task formulations vary significantly across languages. To address this limitation, we introduce a <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality Scoring Model (QSM)</span>, which designed to select high-quality multilingual IFT samples in a language-agnostic manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In parallel, <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">data diversity</span> has been empirically demonstrated to exert a direct and significant impact on LLM performance during IFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. While prior work has focused predominantly on diversity within monolingual (particularly English) datasets, we generalize to multilingual contexts scenarios. To this end, we propose an <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unsupervised clustering algorithm</span> that selects diverse samples in a language-agnostic manner. By analyzing IFT dataset semantic features, this algorithm ensures broad coverage of semantic distribution, thereby enhancing the generalized instruction capacity of trained model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we revisit the Superficial Alignment Hypothesis (SAH), originally proposed in the context of LIMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, which posits that the IFT stage does not impart new knowledge to the model but merely aligns its outputs with human-preferred formats—assuming sufficient prior knowledge acquisition during continuous pretraining (CPT). In this work, we investigate the validity of SAH in multilingual scenarios by analyzing how variations in the scale and composition of the IFT dataset influence downstream performance.</p>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In summary, this work makes the following <span id="S1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">three contributions</span>:</p>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M-DaQ, a novel method for improving LLM multilinguality</span>. This method jointly optimizes both sample quality (via QSM) and diversity (via unsupervised clustering algorithm) for Multilingual IFT dataset. We will open-source code to facilitate reproducibility and future research.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a comprehensive <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multilingual IFT evaluation across 18 languages</span>. Our results demonstrate that M-DaQ effectively mitigates multilingual challenges arising from the scarcity of high-quality IFT data and the underrepresentation of culturally specific values.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the first systematic empirical investigation of the <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAH in multilingual contexts</span>. Our findings provide novel insights into how the scale and composition of IFT data influence cross-lingual LLM alignment.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IFT data selection has been investigated in several prior studies<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. For example, Zhao et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> propose heuristic-based selection strategies, while Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> utilize LLMs to score and rank training samples for subsequent selection. However, these methods largely disregard the linguistic and cultural specificities of individual languages. Furthermore, as noted by recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, employing general-purpose LLMs as judges for multilingual data quality remains unreliable due to cross-lingual inconsistencies and evaluation biases. To overcome these limitations, we fine-tune a expert model-QSM specifically designed for multilingual contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Improving multilingual IFT datasets remains a non-trivial challenge. Current approaches predominantly rely on machine translation (MT) to convert high-quality English IFT data into other languages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. While scalable, this strategy inevitably introduces translation errors and fails to generate culturally appropriate or contextually grounded responses, which are critical factors for real-world multilingual applications. Alternative methods, such as MIDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, attempt to manually or semi-automatically adapt instructions and responses to better suit low-resource languages. Although more linguistically sensitive, such approaches incur significantly higher annotation and training costs, limiting their scalability. Our work directly addresses this underexplored direction by introducing a principled, language-agnostic selection methodology that prioritizes both quality and diversity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15549v1/images/algorithm.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/319;" width="381" height="319" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Overview of the M-DaQ Algorithm. The <span id="S3.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality Scoring Model</span> assigns data quality scores to IFT samples. The <span id="S3.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diversity Control</span> module clusters instructions based on semantic representations, and selects the highest-scoring instance from each cluster to ensure both quality and coverage.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Quality Scoring Model (QSM)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality Scoring Model (QSM)</span>, a fine-tuned variant of XLM-RoBERTa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> designed to estimate the quality of multilingual IFT samples. The model is trained on a curated multilingual dataset derived from MIDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, which comprises 18 languages with approximately 2.3K expert-revised IFT samples per language, originally sourced from the Alpaca dataset. Therefore, QSM is aligned with linguistic</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each instruction <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positive response</span> <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> (high-quality, human-revised) and a <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">negative response</span> <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (low-quality, potentially noisy or machine-generated). The training objective is to maximize the score assigned to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> while minimizing the score for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively learning a relative quality ranking. This is formalized via a <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">triplet loss</span> function that operates in the embedding space:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{triplet}}=\max\left(\|\mathbf{v}_{i}-\mathbf{v}_{p}\|_{2}-\|\mathbf{v}_{i}-\mathbf{v}_{n}\|_{2}+\epsilon,\ 0\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>triplet</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{triplet}}=\max\left(\|\mathbf{v}_{i}-\mathbf{v}_{p}\|_{2}-\|\mathbf{v}_{i}-\mathbf{v}_{n}\|_{2}+\epsilon,\ 0\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the encoded representations of the instruction, positive response, and negative response, respectively; <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a margin hyperparameter enforcing separation between positive and negative pairs. This formulation encourages the model to assign higher similarity scores to high-quality (instruction, response) pairs while pushing apart low-quality ones.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Diversity Selection Algorithm (DSA)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure linguistic and semantic diversity in the selected multilingual dataset, we propose the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diversity Selection Algorithm (DSA)</span>—an unsupervised machine learning method capable of operating across any language without reliance on language-specific resources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">DSA first encodes all IFT samples into a shared multilingual embedding space. We then apply <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Principal Component Analysis (PCA)</span> for dimensionality reduction, followed by clustering (e.g., k-means) to partition the data into semantically coherent groups. Each sample is assigned a cluster identifier, serving as a proxy for its semantic category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The selection procedure is formalized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Diversity Selection Algorithm (DSA) ‣ 3 Method ‣ A METHOD FOR IMPROVING MULTILINGUAL QUALITY AND DIVERSITY OF INSTRUCTION FINE-TUNING DATASETS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. To prioritize high-quality instances, we employ the quality score predicted by the QSM to retain only the top-ranked IFT samples. Semantic clustering is performed by first generating sentence embeddings using XLM-RoBERTa and subsequently assigning cluster labels via k-means clustering. By sampling the highest samples from each cluster equally, we enhance the semantic diversity of the resulting dataset. Furthermore, to ensure adequate instruction complexity, we impose a minimum threshold on the complexity score for sample inclusion. This multi-objective selection strategy thus guarantees that the final dataset is not only of high quality but also broadly representative of both semantic variation and linguistic complexity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> The selection process of DSA</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            IFT Dataset <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> with quality scores, <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{diversity}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>diversity</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{diversity}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{quality}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>quality</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{quality}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Selected subset <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{thres}}\leftarrow\text{avg}(\text{d.complexity}\text{ for }d\in\mathcal{D})\times 0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>thres</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>avg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d.complexity</mtext><mtext> for </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{thres}}\leftarrow\text{avg}(\text{d.complexity}\text{ for }d\in\mathcal{D})\times 0.1</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{sorted}}\leftarrow\text{Sort}(\mathcal{D},\text{key}=\text{quality\_score},\text{descending})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>sorted</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Sort</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>key</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>quality_score</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext>descending</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{sorted}}\leftarrow\text{Sort}(\mathcal{D},\text{key}=\text{quality\_score},\text{descending})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\mathcal{D}_{\text{sorted}}[1:n_{\text{quality}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>sorted</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>quality</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\mathcal{D}_{\text{sorted}}[1:n_{\text{quality}}]</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Top-quality samples
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{diverse\_set}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>diverse_set</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{diverse\_set}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each instruction <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d.\text{cluster}\notin\text{diverse\_set}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>cluster</mtext><mo>∉</mo><mtext>diverse_set</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d.\text{cluster}\notin\text{diverse\_set}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">and</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d.\text{complexity}&gt;c_{\text{thres}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mtext>complexity</mtext><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>thres</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d.\text{complexity}&gt;c_{\text{thres}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\mathcal{S}\cup\{d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\mathcal{S}\cup\{d\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Diverse samples
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{diverse\_set}\leftarrow\text{diverse\_set}\cup\{d.\text{clusters}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>diverse_set</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mtext>diverse_set</mtext><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mtext>clusters</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{diverse\_set}\leftarrow\text{diverse\_set}\cup\{d.\text{clusters}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply our proposed <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M-DaQ</span> algorithm to the Alpaca 52K dataset, extending it to 18 languages. We fine-tune the Llama 3 8B base model using the selected subset, resulting in the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M-DaQ Model</span>. For comparison, we train a <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vanilla Model</span> using the original, unfiltered Alpaca 52K dataset. Both models are trained under identical hyperparameter configurations, which are a batch size of 256, and a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. The only difference lies in training duration: the M-DaQ model is trained for only 150 steps, whereas the Vanilla Model is trained for 10× more steps (1,500 steps), ensuring a fair comparison under constrained computational budgets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Evaluation Protocol</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-as-Judge.</span> We employ an advanced LLM model as the judge model to comparatively evaluate the outputs of the M-DaQ and Vanilla Models. To mitigate judge bias, we adopt the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">win rate<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">[</span><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">]</span></cite></span> — a bidirectional pairwise comparison metric that evaluates each model’s output in both instruction→response and response→instruction directions. We evaluate on two widely used IFT benchmarks: Alpaca-Eval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and MT-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, both of which have been machine-translated and subsequently human-revised for 18 languages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Evaluation.</span> To complement automated evaluation and assess nuanced linguistic and cultural quality, we conduct a large-scale human evaluation with 7 native-speaking language experts, each with an average of 3.9 years of professional experience. For each language and dataset (Alpaca-Eval and MT-Bench), experts performed multi-stage evaluations including sampling, independent rating, cross-review, and rebuttal rounds until consensus was reached. The entire process consumed 58 human-hours. As in LLM-as-Judge, we report performance using the win rate metric.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Results and Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:102pt;vertical-align:-48.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(7.3pt,-2.2pt) scale(1.04448587807134,1.04448587807134) ;">
